WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.040
Depending on who you ask, Marvel's nocturnal
hero Moon Knight is either a mercenary, an

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:11.040
emissary of an ancient Egyptian god, an obvious
Batman clone, or just an absolute lunatic.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.499
Over the years, countless revivals and reinterpretations
have given readers reason to think of him

00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:17.710
in any of those ways and more.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.010
As a side effect, Moon Knight’s origins,
and even the core of his character, can be

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.550
tough to figure out.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.810
Asking a question as simple as "What's Moon
Knight's secret identity?" is complicated

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.810
enough to make you shake your head.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.390
So to help you along, here’s the truth about
Moon Knight.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.370
Moon Knight's real-life namesake

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:40.390
Rather than the usual hero/everyman dichotomy
we're used to with Superman and Clark Kent,

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.100
or Spider-Man and Peter Parker, Moon Knight
has at least three "civilian" names.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:50.539
There's taxi driver Jake Lockley, the mercenary
Marc Spector, and millionaire Steven Grant.

00:00:50.539 --> 00:00:55.079
These three identities not only speak and
act differently, the people in Moon Knight's

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:58.319
life choose who they believe is the dominant
persona.

00:00:58.319 --> 00:01:03.129
His girlfriend Marlene, for instance, only
ever refers to him as Steven, whether or not

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.730
he’s wearing the mask.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:09.060
Co-creator Doug Moench, on the other hand,
cast Moon Knight's dominant persona as Marc

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.330
Spector, a name he got from an employee at
a comic shop that Moench called the best comic

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.730
shop in Manhattan.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:19.350
At the time, he often used the names of people
in his life for new characters, and as he

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.670
says, “one of the guys who worked there
was Marc Spector.”

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:26.010
When the employee asked "When are you going
to name a comic book character after me?",

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:30.611
Moench decided to use his name for a new villain
he was creating for an issue of Werewolf By

00:01:30.611 --> 00:01:31.611
Night.

00:01:31.611 --> 00:01:36.910
With that, Moon Knight got a name, and the
real-life Marc Spector got his wish.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.910
Moon Knight's villainous roots

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:43.741
Moench and artist Don Perlin first brought
Moon Knight to life in 1975's Werewolf by

00:01:43.741 --> 00:01:49.450
Night #32, as a mercenary hired by a clandestine
group known only as the Committee to capture

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.360
a werewolf named Jack Russell.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.830
No, really: like the adorable terrier.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:59.050
It was The Committee who gave Spector his
codename, his costume, and even silver gauntlets

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:02.720
and a truncheon specifically designed to hurt
the savage werewolf.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.370
By the end of the next issue, Moon Knight
showed at least a hint or two of the hero

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:07.390
he would become.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:11.560
While he fulfills his contract by capturing
Russell and bringing him to the Committee,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.300
he also becomes convinced that he's on the
wrong side and helps the werewolf to escape.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.350
It’s not all out of altruism, though.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.560
He made sure to keep the Committee’s money
for himself.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.740
Big Bill's breakout

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:27.780
Bill Sienkiewicz is known among fans for having
one of the most unique art styles in comics.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.069
Over the past 30 years, he’s displayed his
scratchy, innovative, and almost abstract

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:37.260
work on books like New Mutants, Daredevil,
Elektra, and even Batman, but he began his

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.760
comic book career drawing Moon Knight.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.700
Sienkiewicz was only 19 years old when he
brought a portfolio to the offices of DC Comics,

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.740
but unfortunately, that was exactly the wrong
time, as DC was cutting loose many of the

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.430
artists who had been working there for years.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:56.840
Still, his talent was evident, so Batman artist
Neal Adams, one of Sienkiewicz’s artistic

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:01.069
influences, “called up Jim Shooter and said,
“I’ve got this kid fresh off the street

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:02.550
and he draws like me.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.880
Is that a problem?”

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.680
With the common perception that Moon Knight
was Marvel’s Batman, he fit right in alongside

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:09.680
Moench.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.950
The first Jewish superhero?

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.849
Moench told Charlie Huston he “made up the
first Jewish costumed hero,” though he also

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:19.040
says he didn't begin with that in mind.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.590
After learning that the real life Marc Spector
was Jewish, he reasoned that the fictional

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.310
version ought to be as well.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:29.340
Whether Moon Knight really is the first Jewish
superhero depends on a number of things, including

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.990
on how you define "the first Jewish superhero."

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:36.690
Ben Grimm predates Moon Knight by a decade,
though he wasn’t confirmed to be Jewish

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.620
until a 2002 issue of Fantastic Four.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:45.400
Magneto debuted all the way back in 1963,
but his status a Holocaust survivor wouldn’t

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.370
be revealed until 1981.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:53.230
Similarly, Doc Samson’s Jewish heritage
wasn't discussed until 1990, well after the

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:56.050
character’s debut in the pages of Incredible
Hulk.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:04:00.670
So whether or not he was the first, Moon Knight
was the first one whose heritage was discussed

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:01.930
on the page.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:04.099
Either way, it’s a milestone.

00:04:04.099 --> 00:04:05.770
The Hawkeye connection

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:09.810
Moon Knight has never been much of a team
player, but in the late ‘80s, his status

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:14.370
as an up-and-coming fan-favorite character
landed him a spot on the West Coast Avengers

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:18.310
- and gave him a pretty interesting source
for his crime-fighting equipment.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:25.030
In 1987's West Coast Avengers #23, the L.A.-based
team went back in time to ancient Egypt.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:29.280
Once there, they found worshippers of the
moon god Khonshu - the patron diety who brought

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.750
Marc Spector back from the dead and charged
him to fight for justice - under siege.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:38.280
Determined to help the Egyptians defend themselves,
Hawkeye designed weapons for them like darts

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.500
and boomerangs.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:44.530
The issue ends with a cut back to modern times,
where we see Marc Spector finding the same

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.180
weapons his fellow Avenger designed.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:53.090
To date, we don't know if Moon Knight or Khonshu
have offered Hawkeye royalties or even just

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:54.090
a co-creator credit.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:58.100
If not, maybe Clint Barton should think about
retaining the services of Nelson &amp; Murdock

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.880
for the inevitable litigation.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.240
Movie Knight?

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.530
Plans for an adaptation of Moon Knight have
seemed to be in the works for years, mostly

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:07.860
on the small screen.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:12.120
The name “Marc Spector” was mentioned
on an episode of the short-lived Blade television

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:16.730
series, in which Sticky Fingaz from the rap
group Onyx took over for Wesley Snipes.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:21.430
Subsequently, Marvel Studios partnered with
No Equal Entertainment to develop a show based

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:26.330
on the hero, and once Netflix launched their
Defenders series, rumors started swirling

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:31.240
that the Fist of Khonshu would fit right in
with the street-level side of the Marvel Universe.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:36.080
Even recent Moon Knight comics have provided
an episodic, psychological thriller take on

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.070
the hero that would seem like a perfect fit
for a TV show.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:42.450
But maybe fans have been setting their sights
on the small screen when they should’ve

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.030
been looking for something bigger.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:47.919
It's not impossible that the hero could appear
in one of the unnamed Marvel Studios films

00:05:47.919 --> 00:05:51.900
coming up after they wrap up Phase Three with
the Infinity War sequel.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:56.750
In fact, Guardians of the Galaxy director
James Gunn confirmed in January 2017 that

00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:01.110
he has an idea for a Moon Knight film that
he presented to Marvel.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:02.110
Awesome!

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:03.210
Thanks for watching!

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.680
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:07.830
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

